  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946476/assets/aff2ca3dac212832eb3b2219678dab3f4029319ce6b09f8c134f9dcc99b72640.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $13{\\text{\u00a0mi}}^{2}.$   Then, the area of $\u25b3BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is{{field-1}}${\\text{mi}}^{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by finding the area of $\u25b3ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\u25b3ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 6.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061{90}^{\u2218} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 32.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061{90}^{\u2218} \\\\  & \u224816{\\text{\u00a0mi}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest integer**.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $\u25b3ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $\u25b3BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\u25b3BCD} & ={A}_{\u25b3ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =16-13 \\\\  & =3{\\text{\u00a0mi}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
